{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"J5 c97f CLOSED — acceptance evidence is a GREEN CI RUN, not an assertion. Run 30515632763 on merged commit bb951f6e4, ubuntu-latest (native x86_64): built from the vendored Dockerfile, 'Jira DC ready after 37 tries' (~3 min), '4 passed in 6.39s', clean teardown, failure-logs step skipped. Re-verified after adding the freshness guard (run 30515632763) because that guard runs in the SESSION FIXTURE before any test — a prior green could not cover code that did not exist yet.\nThe close took two extra hoops, both worth recording:\n 1. The completion verifier CORRECTLY refused an AC that no longer matched the code: 'the pre-existing LLM-keyed job is unmodified', when I had deliberately added --ignore=tests/external/live_jira_dc to it. The AC was wrong, not the code — the flag is required because tests/external/conftest.py's all-skip canary counts jira_live items GLOBALLY, so without it the DC tests' skipping is masked by the Cloud tests that execute, costing the canary the exact detection it exists for. Corrected the AC to record that single deliberate exception rather than leave it contradicting the code.\n 2. Editing the AC staled the plan attestation, so the close gate demanded a fresh review-plan (PASS, 0 blocking) before it would proceed. Then the d59e context-bound bug bit twice more; a retry cleared it, exactly as that bug's own H2 mechanism predicts (recovery is entered only when the PRIMARY verifier truncates, which is a property of the live call, not the ticket).\n\nd59e FIXED AND MERGED (change 1027). Root-caused via /rebar-debug under the full protocol. Two of the three confirmed mechanisms were INTENDED behaviour and left untouched: the 24,000-char bound (a documented fail-closed cost safeguard pinned by a test asserting ZERO billable recovery calls) and the fallback-only-on-truncation gating (which is what makes the symptom look flaky). Only the misattributed MESSAGE was a defect. The operator now gets the measured size against the limit and a pointer to the gate_error_v1 sidecar, while a genuine unavailability still gets the install-the-extra guidance — pinned by a negative control so narrowing cannot decay into deleting.\nThat fix also took a correction I want recorded: my first split put the whole CLI message in rebar/llm/failure.py, which was the wrong abstraction (its neighbour message_for returns a FRAGMENT) and leaked the close command's concerns into a generic module — and I had chosen it to save lines under the 800-LOC cap. Redone so failure.py owns only the cause explanation and the caller owns its phrasing; the unification into ONE message with a variable remedy was both more readable and smaller, so the cap stopped driving the design.\nCI GATE CAUGHT ME: inlining the branch pushed transition_close.py from 791 to 829 LOC, failing the module-size gate on all four matrix entries. I had briefed the J5 implementer about exactly that cap and did not apply it to my own edit. Held-out oracles cannot catch that class — only the project's structural gates can.\n\nBUG FILED: 8070-3f8b-8e71-483b (perky-soapstone-stork) — the external-integration weekly canary has been RED since 2026-07-14 (last green), failing identically on the 07-20 and 07-27 CRONS with SnapshotRefError on HEAD across ~7 live tests (35 occurrences in one run). Explicitly NOT caused by this epic: the jira DC harness job added on 07-30 PASSES, and the two failing jobs failed before it existed. Checked for a duplicate first — 11 open bugs, zero matching, though three CLOSED bugs sit in the same family and are named as possible-regression reading.\n\nJ6 9fd4 PLAN REVIEW #1 -> BLOCK (2 blocking, 11 advisory), both blockers impact 1.0 and both verified by me in the tree:\n B1: Scope omitted adapters/__init__.py. Importing an adapter's backend module is the SIDE EFFECT that calls register() into _backend_registry (see the existing 'from .jira import backend as _jira_backend' at line 15), so without the DC equivalent select_backend('jira-datacenter') raises KeyError at runtime with every other file correct — and no unit test of the transport would catch it.\n B2: Scope said tests/live_jira_dc/test_transport.py. That directory does not exist; J5 landed the harness at tests/external/live_jira_dc/. I had found this independently before the review returned. It is not cosmetic — outside tests/external/ a live test is collected by the default suite AND the per-commit integration step with no Docker, the same trap I fixed inside J5.\n Also applied: an AC for the NameIdentity user-search lookup (the Approach promised it, nothing verified it — leaving it unwired would restore the permanently-non-authoritative assignee J4's anti-churn oracle exists to prevent), _config_schema.py added to Scope (its own ACs and the epic already assigned it), an AC pinning select_backend('jira-datacenter') resolves, and the tier obligations J5 established (external auto-marker, REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL opt-in, and a required _live_jira_ready sentinel so J6's live tests join the all-skip canary instead of silently opting out).\nReview #2 running."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785427013296078001,"uuid":"2803b58a-5dd2-4cc1-b209-9eaf5a0c4672"}